Hard irregular prostate nodule, Elevated PSA, Insidious lower urinary tract symptoms, Aching bone pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GEF-040
Reason for Visit: Severe, unremitting lower back pain resulting in an acute inability to walk, accompanied by recent weight loss and urinary symptoms.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an elderly male patient with severe, new-onset, unremitting back pain that is preventing mobilization, set against a background of insidious weight loss and urinary hesitancy. Urgent clinical assessment was mandated to rule out acute spinal cord compression (a true oncological/neurological emergency) and provide immediate severe pain control prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My lower back is aching so deeply I can't sleep or stand up, and I've been struggling to pass urine for months."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an insidious onset of progressive lower urinary tract symptoms over the past year, including hesitancy, a weak stream, and increased frequency, which he previously attributed to "getting older" and never had investigated (frequently completely asymptomatic early presentation). Over the past four weeks, he has developed a deep, unremitting aching bone pain in the lower back and pelvis. This pain has acutely worsened over the last 48 hours, rendering him unable to walk or find a comfortable sleeping position. He reports unintentional significant weight loss (6kg) and profound anorexia over the last two months. He denies microscopic or macroscopic haematuria. He reports recent erectile dysfunction. Crucially, he denies new-onset neurological deficits such as loss of bowel/bladder control, saddle anaesthesia, or sudden bilateral leg weakness (signs of severe spinal cord compression). He notes mild bilateral lower extremity oedema recently.
Functional Status: Acutely bedbound due to severe bony pain; previously fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of pathological fractures, severe unmanaged pain, and delayed oncological diagnosis.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 155/92 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Visibly pale, cachectic, and in severe distress, lying rigidly in bed.
Physical Exam: Digital rectal examination (DRE) reveals a hard, highly irregular, asymmetrical, and nodular prostate gland. Abdominal exam is soft with a slightly palpable, distended bladder. Musculoskeletal exam of the spine reveals profound, deep tenderness over the lumbar vertebrae and sacroiliac joints, without focal superficial muscle spasm. Neurological examination of the lower extremities reveals normal 5/5 motor strength, intact sensation, and normal reflexes; anal sphincter tone is intact, currently ruling out acute spinal cord compression. Bilateral mild lower extremity pitting oedema is present. (An elevated serum PSA and identification of malignant adenocarcinoma on biopsy are anticipated diagnostic tetrad findings).
Environmental Observations: Patient has been using a heat pack continuously, which provides zero relief for the deep bony pain.
Medication Reconciliation: Only taking paracetamol, which is completely ineffective for this level of severe oncological/bony pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Suspected Advanced Prostate Cancer with likely skeletal metastases - Status: Acute Pain Crisis
Differentials:

Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH) with acute urinary retention - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While BPH causes urinary symptoms, the DRE reveals a hard, nodular, asymmetrical gland characteristic of malignancy, not the smooth, symmetrical enlargement of BPH. Furthermore, BPH does not cause destructive, unremitting bony back pain or profound weight loss.

Osteoarthritis of the Lumbar Spine - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The pain is unremitting, deep, and present at rest/night, rather than mechanical pain that worsens with activity and relieves with rest. The associated systemic signs (weight loss, nodular prostate) point away from a simple degenerative condition.

Multiple Myeloma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: While it causes bony pain and weight loss, the explicit presence of the hard, nodular prostate on physical exam makes metastatic prostate cancer the overwhelmingly most likely primary source.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme (High risk of spinal cord compression and immense suffering)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with classic 'red flag' symptoms for advanced, metastatic prostate cancer: a hard, nodular prostate, insidious lower urinary tract symptoms, profound weight loss, and severe, unremitting bony back pain. Telephone triage appropriately identified the urgent need to rule out impending spinal cord compression. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have resulted in agonizing, unmanaged oncological pain, extreme distress, and the risk of missing early neurological signs of cord compression. This urgent after-hours intervention successfully performed the critical DRE to identify the likely primary source, definitively ruled out an immediate neurosurgical emergency (cord compression), provided immediate potent opioid analgesia, and orchestrated an urgent, targeted rapid-access oncological workup for the following day.

